      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, my apologies for the confusion! You're right; the Chicago Manual of Style does indeed have a different approach when it comes to citing works with more than three authors. In Chicago, you would list all authors the first time you cite the work. So, if you have a paper with five authors, you would cite it as Smith, Johnson, Brown, Davis, and Wilson (2020). However, on subsequent citations, you can shorten it to Smith et al. (2020).\n\nSo, to clarify, in Chicago, you do not use \"et al.\" in the text for subsequent citations unless you are citing a work with more than three authors. It's like saying, \"The whole gang was in on it, but we can just refer to the leader when we're in a hurry, unless we're talking about a larger group, in which case we mention everyone by name.\"\n\nI hope this clears up any confusion! Remember, these citation styles are like different tribes of the scholarly world, each with their own customs and traditions. Stick to the rules of the tribe you're working with, and you'll be fine!",
